---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import PageShell from '../../components/nav/PageShell.astro';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import { CRATE_URL, DOCSRS_URL } from '../../consts';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'How QCL works | DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'A QCL program configures a subject, validates it with checks that report what they examined, and controls it with experiments the planner chooses. Failures name what broke.';

const shape = `config(subject, candidates)  →  validate(checks)  →  Screened  →  control(evidence)  →  report`;

const vacuous = `check_markov         Accepted  examined 1  worst margin 0.000e0
check_decomposable   Vacuous   examined 0  worst margin 0.000e0`;

const failure = `[2] Non-commuting model: σx and σz on leg 0
    ✓ validate failed: factors at nodes 0 and 1 do not commute
    the subject is as built: is_frozen() = true

[3] The same non-commuting model on a dynamic graph, through the shipped freeze
    ✓ freeze aborted: factors at nodes 0 and 1 do not commute
    is_frozen() = false (rolled back)`;

const law = `[Z̄(0)] at the shipped precisions
         f32: e1 = 1.19e-6, e2 = 4.77e-7, |t1|_pre = 1.000000, |t2|_post = 2.000000, bound = 2.86e-6, measured = 9.54e-7, holds
         f64: e1 = 2.22e-15, e2 = 1.78e-15, |t1|_pre = 1.000000, |t2|_post = 2.000000, bound = 6.22e-15, measured = 1.78e-15, holds
    Float106: e1 = 0.00e0, e2 = 0.00e0, |t1|_pre = 1.000000, |t2|_post = 2.000000, bound = 0.00e0, measured = 0.00e0, holds`;
---
<PageShell title={title} description={description} path="/how-it-works/">
  <header class="doc-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
    <h1>A QCL program configures, validates and controls.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      QCL, the Quantum Causal Language, is a builder in Rust. You name a subject, the builder checks
      it, and what passes goes to a stage that plans experiments and reads their results. Every
      check reports what it examined, and every failure names what broke.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="shape">
    <h2>What is the shape of a program?</h2>
    <p>Every program starts in one place and moves in one direction.</p>
    <Code code={shape} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} wrap />
    <p>
      <code>QclBuilder::config</code> names two working types once: a real type for accuracy and a
      count type for headroom. <code>build()</code> refuses what would answer unsoundly, before any
      stage runs. <code>validate</code> ends in a <code>Screened</code> value. A structural
      candidate reaches <code>control</code> only through that value, so no path skips the checks,
      and the compiler enforces it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pipeline adds order and names to the causal monad the crate is built on. It adds no new
      monad.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="subjects">
    <h2>What can you name as the subject?</h2>
    <p>The subject decides which checks exist. There are four.</p>
    <dl class="deflist">
      <div>
        <dt>Plant</dt>
        <dd>
          A system that evolves and is measured, with the observables it exposes and its
          candidates. A candidate is structural, which claims a causal structure and goes through
          the screen, or a mechanism, which claims none and enters control directly. Checks:
          <code>check_markov</code>, <code>check_decomposable</code>. Example:
          <a href="/examples/qcl-crosstalk/">crosstalk</a>.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Model</dt>
        <dd>
          A Choi–Jamiołkowski factorization over a frozen graph. It is a plant with one structural
          candidate and no evidence, and the shape the existing freeze check maps onto. Checks:
          <code>check_markov</code>, <code>check_decomposable</code>. Example:
          <a href="/examples/qcl-qcm-freeze/">freeze</a>.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Code</dt>
        <dd>
          A chain complex, evaluated exactly. It has no experiments, no baseline and no evidence,
          so it offers validate stages only: <code>derive_code</code>,
          <code>check_ldpc_weights</code>, <code>check_class_invariance</code>,
          <code>check_clifford_action</code>. Example:
          <a href="/examples/qcl-geometric-qec/">toric code</a>.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Circuit</dt>
        <dd>
          A compositional model. The circuit is the stored object, and its dilation is what the
          structural stages screen. It is the one subject that can enter an abstraction. Checks:
          <code>check_markov</code>, <code>check_decomposable</code>,
          <code>check_alignment_structure</code>, <code>check_naturality</code>. Example:
          <a href="/examples/qcl-crosstalk-circuits/">crosstalk over circuits</a>.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="checks">
    <h2>What does a check report?</h2>
    <p>
      Every check returns one shape: what it measured, its threshold, the margin between them, and
      how many items it examined. The verdict is Accepted, Rejected or Vacuous. Vacuous means that
      nothing was examined, so nothing was certified.
    </p>
    <p>
      A bare pass would hide that case. On a two-node chain no 3×3 sub-relation exists, so the
      decomposability check has nothing to search, and the report says so.
    </p>
    <Code code={vacuous} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} wrap />
    <p>
      Tolerances form one family with named members: commutator, validation, numerical rank,
      state and shot noise. All but shot noise derive from the machine epsilon of the working
      real type, so moving from <code>f64</code> to <code>f32</code> moves those thresholds with
      it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A computed rejection has three outcomes, and they depend on the stage and the subject. On a
      plant, <code>check_markov</code> and <code>check_decomposable</code> drop a failing
      candidate from the admitted set. On a model or a circuit, a rejection from those two
      checks is the stage’s failure and <code>finalize()</code> returns the error. The three code
      checks record the rejection in the report, and <code>finalize()</code> returns
      <code>Ok</code>. A structural error, such as a shape mismatch, fails the stage in every
      case.
    </p>
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href="/checks/">Every check, and what backs it →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="control">
    <h2>What does control do?</h2>
    <p>
      Control turns a screened set of candidates into a decision. Its stages read like verbs, and
      two orders are valid. A mechanism plant runs <code>fork</code>, <code>observe</code>,
      <code>gate</code>, <code>adjudicate</code>. A structural plant runs <code>observe</code>,
      <code>fork</code>, <code>predict</code>, <code>compare</code>, <code>adjudicate</code>.
    </p>
    <dl class="deflist">
      <div><dt><code>fork</code></dt><dd>Forks one world per candidate, each with its own ledger. A structural candidate’s world holds the plant as it is, and a mechanism candidate’s world holds the plant evolved by that candidate’s channel.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>observe</code></dt><dd>Reads an observable over a number of shots.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>predict</code></dt><dd>Reads each world’s predicted value for an observable.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>compare</code></dt><dd>Judges each world’s prediction against the observation, in standard errors.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>gate</code></dt><dd>Judges a read-out against a specification: at least a value, or at most one.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>design</code></dt><dd>Solves the minimum-cost cover: the cheapest experiments that separate each coverable pair of candidates by a floor of bits, and lists the pairs no experiment reaches.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>adjudicate</code></dt><dd>Folds the verdicts and names a survivor, or names why the data leaves several standing.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      The ledger counts shots, experiments, predictions, device time, cost and bits. A budget
      draws down through checked subtraction, and an overdrawn budget returns an error that names
      the shortfall.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="failure">
    <h2>What happens when something fails?</h2>
    <p>
      The error names the offender. The two node indices of a non-commuting pair and the
      generator whose image leaves the code space are typed fields. The triples of a C₃ and the
      name of a cyclic candidate sit in the error’s message. A stage mutates nothing, so a failed <code>validate</code> leaves the subject as it
      was built. A failed freeze rolls the graph back to its dynamic state, so a model that cannot
      freeze is never left half frozen.
    </p>
    <Code code={failure} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} wrap />
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href={`${CRATE_URL}/src/error/quantum_error.rs`}>Every error variant in the source ↗</a>
      <a href={DOCSRS_URL}>API reference on docs.rs ↗</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="precision">
    <h2>How do you choose the precision?</h2>
    <p>
      With one type alias. Each example declares <code>type FloatType = Float106;</code>, and the
      factors, the separations, the plan’s costs and every read-out recompute at that precision.
      Switch it to <code>f32</code>, <code>f64</code> or <code>BFloat16</code> and no other line
      of source changes. The results and thresholds move, and some examples state where a narrow
      type stops meeting their tolerance. A hard-coded <code>f64</code> anywhere in a program stays invisible while the alias
      is <code>f64</code> and fails to compile the moment the two differ, which is why the
      examples default to <code>Float106</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="abstractions">
    <h2>What is an abstraction, and what does QCL do with one?</h2>
    <p>
      An abstraction relates a detailed model to a coarser one. Eight physical qubits carry two
      logical qubits, and the logical model describes the same behaviour with fewer parts. Lorenz
      and Tull (arXiv:2602.16612) define when such a map is sound: it must commute with every
      query in a chosen signature.
    </p>
    <p>
      QCL measures that commutation as a residual, so an approximate abstraction reports how far
      it sits from exact. When two abstractions compose, a law bounds the composite:
      <code>ε ≤ ‖τ₂‖_post · ε₁ + ‖τ₁‖_pre · ε₂</code>. The crate computes both norms for each
      query. In the Frobenius norm on Choi operators neither equals 1 in general, because a
      partial trace over a factor of dimension d has norm √d.
    </p>
    <p>
      The concatenated-code example prints the law at three precisions. The measured residual sits
      under the bound at each.
    </p>
    <Code code={law} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} />
    <p>
      When both residuals are zero the composite is exact, and Lean proves that case
      (<code>quantum.abstraction.compose_exact</code>). The bound for nonzero residuals follows
      from the triangle inequality and has no Lean statement.
    </p>
    <p>
      A decoder is an abstraction too, from a circuit to its detector error model. The crate
      contains no decoding algorithm. The decoder’s reading of the record enters as data, and the
      library checks the model against the circuit by injecting a fault at each named location. A
      correlated error the model omits shows as the worst failure, and the failure names the
      location. Tests cover this path, and no example runs it.
    </p>
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href="/examples/qcl-concatenated-code/">Concatenated code example →</a>
      <a href="/examples/qcl-code-switching/">Code switching example →</a>
      <a href="/examples/qcl-distillation-round/">Distillation example →</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</PageShell>
